{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 176 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "bf1764a7987e3195295b813e3bc48904e6d61c075826d4484ffcebfb01ae27af" :edb/db-before-t 175 :edb/db-after-t 176 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533488 50 #inst "2026-10-11T02:47:40.356Z" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186060278 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058260 13194139533488 17592186045585 false] [17592186060278 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402375 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186060278 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186060278 1071 "The angle $\\theta =-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186060278 1071 "The angle $θ =-\\frac{9π}{4}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" 13194139533488 17592186045585 false] [17592186060278 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ by *adding* integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n- $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}+2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$\n\n- $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ is coterminal with $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/89/891959642b0d055d852329c59be7541179dd5ae0c4182c2bec533261ce3a700d.png)\n\nWe see that $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ also lies in this quadrant." 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186069274 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066784 13194139533488 17592186045585 false] [17592186069274 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402362 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186069274 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186069274 1071 "The equation $z^{2} - 50 =-10z$ can be written in the form $(z + p)^{2} = q$. Find $p + q$, and solve the equation." 13194139533488 17592186045585 false] [17592186069274 1071 "The equation ${z}^{2}-50=-10z$ can be written in the form $(z+p{)}^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186069274 1074 "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, the $-10z$ term to the left-hand side, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-50 & =-10z \\\\ {z}^{2}+10z & =50 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot z & =50 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot z+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =50 \\\\ (z+5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =50 \\\\ (z+5{)}^{2} & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=5,q=75,$ and $p+q=80.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+5{)}^{2} & =75 \\\\ \\sqrt{(z+5{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\ |z+5| & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\ z+5 & =\\pm 5\\sqrt{3} \\\\ z & =-5\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186105863 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402408 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186119818 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402368 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186119818 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402395 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186119843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402262 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124481 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402433 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124486 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402454 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124486 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402467 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124550 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402474 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124562 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402447 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124681 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402139 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124681 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402146 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124681 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402153 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121880 13194139533488 17592186045585 false] [17592186124885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402402 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124885 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124885 1071 "The projection of the intersection between the elliptic cylinder $9x^{2} + 2(y - 1)^{2} = 18$ and the plane $z = 1$ onto the $xy$-plane is an ellipse. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the ellipse?" 13194139533488 17592186045585 false] [17592186124885 1071 "The projection of the intersection between the elliptic cylinder $9{x}^{2}+2(y-1)^{2}=18$ and the plane $z=1$ onto the $xy$-plane is an ellipse. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the ellipse?" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124885 1074 "To find the intersection of the cylinder and the plane $z=1,$ we substitute $z=1$ into the equation of the cylinder. However, since the equation has no (explicit) $z$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+2(y-1)^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{(3)^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an ellipse with semi-axes of lengths $\\sqrt{2}$ and $3.$" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124888 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121898 13194139533488 17592186045585 false] [17592186124888 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402415 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124888 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124888 1071 "The projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{x^{2}}{8} + ((y - 4)^{2})/(18) - \\frac{z^{2}}{50} = 1$ and the plane $x = 2$ onto the $yz$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?" 13194139533488 17592186045585 false] [17592186124888 1071 "The projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{50}=1$ and the plane $x=2$ onto the $yz$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124888 1074 "To find the intersection of the hyperboloid and the plane $x=2$, we substitute $x=2$ into the equation of the hyperboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{50} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{50} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{50} & =1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{50} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbola with semi-axes of lengths $3$ and $5.$" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121904 13194139533488 17592186045585 false] [17592186124889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402421 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124889 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124889 1071 "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $((x - 2)^{2})/(4) + \\frac{y^{2}}{16} - \\frac{z^{2}}{5} = 1$, find its $x$-intercepts." 13194139533488 17592186045585 false] [17592186124889 1071 "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{5}=1$, find its $x$-intercepts." 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124889 1074 "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{0}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $x=0$ and $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0).$" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124892 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121922 13194139533488 17592186045585 false] [17592186124892 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402427 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124892 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124892 1071 "Given the ellipsoid $((x - 2)^{2})/(12) + ((y + 1)^{2})/(6) + ((z - 1)^{2})/(2) = 1$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." 13194139533488 17592186045585 false] [17592186124892 1071 "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{2}=1$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124892 1074 "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(0+1)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{12} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $x=0$ and $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0).$" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124911 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122036 13194139533488 17592186045585 false] [17592186124911 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402461 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124911 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124911 1071 "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $A$, given by\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139533488 17592186045585 false] [17592186124911 1071 "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186124911 1074 "First, we need to convert $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{4} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $A$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns." 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186147516 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402381 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186152282 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402248 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186152282 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402255 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186152287 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402213 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186152288 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402220 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186166935 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402174 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186166967 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402290 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186166967 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402308 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186166967 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402326 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186166967 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402344 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186176091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402227 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186176091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402234 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186176092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402241 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186184173 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402440 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186221418 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402160 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186221418 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402167 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186221457 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402269 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186229133 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402132 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186229148 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402119 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186229194 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402388 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186229675 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228214 13194139533488 17592186045585 false] [17592186229675 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402126 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186229675 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186229675 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186229675 1071 "${x^{′}(t) =-4y(t); y^{′}(t) = 4x(t)$\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: $e^{At} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (4t) & -\\sin (4t) \\\\ \\sin (4t) & \\cos (4t)\\end{bmatrix}$, where $A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.*" 13194139533488 17592186045585 false] [17592186229675 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402119 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402120 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402119 1069 "q-333856" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402119 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402119 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$*" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402119 1072 #uuid "3c065292-5916-5335-a2ce-48232ddb8e4c" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402119 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402120 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402121 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402120 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402122 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402120 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402123 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402120 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402124 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402120 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402125 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402120 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402120 1077 "selection" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402120 1079 #edb/ref 17592186402123 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402120 1080 #uuid "d370b658-9d56-5718-913a-a06cb231e944" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402121 1081 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402121 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402121 1084 #uuid "fa2879fd-b388-54d3-91a6-9889b3cc921c" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402122 1081 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402122 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402122 1084 #uuid "7f291e69-4dda-53a6-8a62-f0061587ecc0" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402123 1081 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{