] [17592186402127 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402127 1084 #uuid "3be54f80-3e00-5c60-8d2c-859b73f1b04a" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402128 1081 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402128 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402128 1084 #uuid "a489112a-2e25-5139-8701-0da71be4eaed" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402129 1081 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402129 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402129 1084 #uuid "f40839a9-1ee4-555c-b0e0-836cd8ef8a5d" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402130 1081 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402130 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402130 1084 #uuid "d13024ed-e948-5192-b456-f8fc65a79957" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402131 1081 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402131 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402131 1084 #uuid "2d239718-30d6-534b-aa0f-8cec27c7ffe8" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402132 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402133 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402132 1069 "q-335312" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402132 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402132 1071 "A rock of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the rock's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after the fall began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the rock is proportional to the square of the rock's velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nWhich differential equation best models the velocity? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, the acceleration due to gravity is $g,$ and the only forces acting on the rock are the weight due to gravity and drag." 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402132 1072 #uuid "28461e48-9c29-58e8-b99d-2d64a173813c" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402132 1074 "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nIn our case, two forces are acting on the rock:\n\n- The force due to gravity, given by the weight $w$ of the rock, which equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =mg \\\\  & =2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The force due to drag (air resistance) $D,$ which is proportional to the square of the velocity of the rock (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Note that, since drag, in $\\text{N}=\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ is the product of the constant $k$ and squared velocity, in $(\\text{m/s})^{2}={\\text{m}}^{2}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{N}}{{\\text{m}}^{2}/{\\text{s}}^{2}}=\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2}}{{\\text{m}}^{2}/{\\text{s}}^{2}}=\\text{kg/m}.\n$$\n\nSumming the forces, the resultant force acting on the rock is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=2g-k{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the rock, we get\n\n$$\n2g-k{v}^{2}=2\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-\\frac{k}{2}{v}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402133 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402134 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402133 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402135 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402133 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402136 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402133 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402137 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402133 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402138 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402133 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402133 1077 "selection" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402133 1079 #edb/ref 17592186402137 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402133 1080 #uuid "8915c372-42a6-5420-8c91-5f069925b152" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402134 1081 "\\frac{dv}{dt}=2g-kv" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402134 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402134 1084 #uuid "32063b22-d699-5f63-a71d-869c73971f55" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402135 1081 "\\frac{dv}{dt}=2g" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402135 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402135 1084 #uuid "0fc50789-5421-50d2-b60e-67a3bd2e10f6" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402136 1081 "\\frac{dv}{dt}=2g-k{v}^{2}" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402136 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402136 1084 #uuid "491ac020-a720-59c1-ba10-c392f69847b9" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402137 1081 "\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-\\frac{k}{2}{v}^{2}" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402137 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402137 1084 #uuid "4948e46f-d047-5768-bf2d-e94a4743506f" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402138 1081 "\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-\\frac{k}{2}v" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402138 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402138 1084 #uuid "d34ed5ad-0c4e-5b97-a8e7-e19f41dfe4f5" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402140 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402139 1069 "q-335542" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402139 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402139 1071 "A wildlife reserve can support a total of $800$ deer.\nAt the beginning of a disease study in the reserve, a parasitic infection has affected $80$ deer. After $10$ days, the number of deer affected by the parasite increases to $300.$ To the nearest day, how long does it take for the infected deer population to reach $600$ animals?\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of deer affected by the parasite, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time, in days.*" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402139 1072 #uuid "a4fd0915-73cc-575a-b3cc-4a156a96dd49" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402139 1074 "The rate at which the deer population affected by the parasite, $P(t),$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total deer population and its current population affected. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the total deer population in the reserve, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=800$ deer, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(800-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{800-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{800-P} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|800-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $800-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 800-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 800-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =800-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial deer population affected by the parasite was $80$ animals. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=80,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =80 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{0} & =80 \\\\ 800-K & =80 \\\\ K & =720\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=800-720{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $10$ days, the deer population affected by the parasite was $300$ animals. So, applying the condition $P(10)=300,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =300 \\\\ 800-720{e}^{-10k} & =300 \\\\ 500 & =720{e}^{-10k} \\\\ {e}^{-10k} & =\\frac{500}{720}=\\frac{25}{36} \\\\ -10k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when $600$ deer are affected by the parasite. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =600 \\\\ 800-720{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =600 \\\\ 200 & =720{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{200}{720}=\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that $600$ deer will be affected by the parasite after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18})}{-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36})}=35.128…≈35\\,\\text{days},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day." 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402140 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402141 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402140 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402142 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [175921864021