f 17592186045486 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402146 1071 "A small bakery produces loaves of bread. The owner knows that the bakery can produce a maximum of $600$ loaves a day; however, current production is below this maximum. Since she started monitoring, the bakers produced $480$ loaves and $500$ loaves on days $4$ and $8,$ respectively. How many loaves do they produce on day $40?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the bread production $E(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402146 1072 #uuid "ee0a87d3-d3e9-539a-9074-68a374d85d6b" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402146 1074 "The rate at which the bread production $E(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum possible bread production and its current bread production. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(a-E),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum production of the bakery, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=600$ loaves, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(600-E).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dE}{600-E} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dE}{600-E} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|600-E| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $600-E>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $E(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}600-E & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 600-E & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 600-E & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ E & =600-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on day $4,$ the bread production was $480$ loaves. So, applying the condition $E(4)=480,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(4) & =480 \\\\ 600-K{e}^{-4k} & =480 \\\\ K{e}^{-4k} & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on day $8,$ the bread production was $500$ loaves. So, applying the condition $E(8)=500,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(8) & =500 \\\\ 600-K{e}^{-8k} & =500 \\\\ K{e}^{-8k} & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-4k}}{K{e}^{-8k}} & =\\frac{120}{100} \\\\ {e}^{4k} & =\\frac{6}{5} \\\\ 4k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6}{5}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-4\\cdot (1/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)} & =120 \\\\ K & =120{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)} \\\\ K & =144\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $E(t):$\n\n$$\nE(t)=600-144{e}^{-(t/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)}\n$$\n\nFinally, the number of loaves baked on day $t=40$ is\n\n$$\nE(40)=600-144{e}^{-(40/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)}≈576.743…≈577\\,\\text{loaves},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402147 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402148 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402147 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402149 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402147 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402150 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402147 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402151 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402147 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402152 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402147 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402147 1077 "selection" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402147 1079 #edb/ref 17592186402150 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402147 1080 #uuid "6e99660a-207e-56cc-af0c-7810e8c04e78" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402148 1081 "$570$ loaves" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402148 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402148 1084 #uuid "a62be6e9-5300-5837-bb21-616f9afabd11" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402149 1081 "$560$ loaves" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402149 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402149 1084 #uuid "52f73195-6ad4-5063-9185-1d0ce506d0fb" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402150 1081 "$577$ loaves" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402150 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402150 1084 #uuid "487b0c65-385a-5896-a74a-7185c8309375" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402151 1081 "$550$ loaves" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402151 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402151 1084 #uuid "cfb7ad78-9e83-5378-a008-fb209e8c0c15" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402152 1081 "$590$ loaves" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402152 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402152 1084 #uuid "6b3362f7-e0ab-59c9-813b-230e3fd73524" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402154 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402153 1069 "q-335619" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402153 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402153 1071 "A national park is monitoring the number of people who have downloaded a new park app. The system can support a maximum of $800$ users, but the total is still below this limit. Since the park began tracking downloads, the number of users reached $620$ on day $3$ and $720$ on day $9.$ On what day will the number of users reach $760?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of users, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402153 1072 #uuid "44cded13-6bc7-52ea-8fcc-d6fe583c3d07" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402153 1074 "The rate at which the number of app users $P(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum population and its current population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of users the system can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=800$ users, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(800-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{800-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{800-P} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|800-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $800-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 800-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 800-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =800-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on day $3,$ the number of users was $620.$ So, applying the condition $P(3)=620,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =620 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{-3k} & =620 \\\\ K{e}^{-3k} & =180.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on day $9,$ the number of users was $720.$ So, applying the condition $P(9)=720,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(9) & =720 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{-9k} & =720 \\\\ K{e}^{-9k} & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-3k}}{K{e}^{-9k}} & =\\frac{180}{80} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-3\\cdot (1/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)} & =180 \\\\ K & =180{e}^{(1/2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)} \\\\ K & =180\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\ K & =180\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ K & =270\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=800-270{e}^{-(t/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $d$  be the day when the app reaches $760$ users. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(d) & =760 \\\\ 800-270{e}^{-kd} & =760 \\\\ 40 & =270{e}^{-kd} \\\\ {e}^{-kd} & =\\frac{40}{270} \\\\ {e}^{-kd} & =\\frac{4}{27} \\\\ -kd & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{27}) \\\\ d & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{27}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the number of users will be $760$ on day\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{27})}{\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4})}=14.1285…≈14,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day." 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402154 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402155 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402154 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402156 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402154 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402157 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402154 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402158 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402154 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402159 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402154 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402154 1077 "selection" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402154 1079 #edb/ref 17592186402156 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402154 1080 #uuid "450a3a23-c54b-5ed0-90a2-deef088dea63" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402155 1081 "$25\\text{th}$ day" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402155 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402155 1084 #uuid "67f7ba4e-e115-562d-8f0b-e2fb3c50999d" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402156 1081 "$14\\text{th}$ day" 13194139533488 17592186045585 true] [17592186402156 1083 #